{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjFrBbyyC44PG4AbPdXk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QFMCU9b3jbLJKP29QbMRQi","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The clef is followed by two flats. The first bar holds two F3 quarters with tremolo strokes, which together are the edition's F3 half with tremolo. Next come an F3 16th beamed to three F2 16ths, then an F2 quarter with a tremolo stroke. That is bar 21 (F3 half tremolo, F3 16th, F2 16th ×3, F2 quarter tremolo). Bar 21 is complete here, so this line begins with bar 21, not bar 20. Bar 20, which is only F3 tremolos, does not appear. The second bar holds four F2 quarters with tremolo strokes. That is bar 22, F2 half tremolo twice. The third bar opens with three beamed 8ths, Bb2 Bb2 C3, and a fourth 8th, C2, below the staff on ledger lines. Then come four F2 16ths, an F2 8th, the repeat sign and an 8th rest, up to the barline. That is bar 23, with the repeat falling inside the bar as the edition says. The line ends at the end of bar 23. It holds bars 21–23.","right":false,"insights":"The line was easy to match because of one distinctive feature. Bar 21 drops from F3 to F2 with a 16th-note run, which bar 20 lacks. The copyist wrote the half-note tremolos as pairs of quarter tremolos, so the line looks like one bar more than it is. The program probably shifted by a bar because bars 20–22 are all repeated F tremolos. Counting beats per bar, and knowing where the previous line ends, would have settled this."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5457,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5457},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":